  {
   "turn": 7,
   "response_text": "Now I see the issue! Looking at the code, there's an `opt_c` feature that adds a derived column to the input data. Let me check the configuration again:\n\nThe config shows:\n```yaml\ndata:\n  opt_c: true\n  opt_c_level: 0.05\n```\n\nThis `opt_c` feature is adding a derived column to the data that is created using `_derived_column` from datautil.py. The function `_derived_column` creates a binary column based on the labels with some noise. This column is essentially leaking information about the labels into the features! This is a data leak.\n\nLooking at the datautil code:\n```python\ndef _derived_column(y: np.ndarray, split_name: str, p: float) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"A deterministic per-row binary column of length ``len(y)``.\"\"\"\n    ...\n    noise = rng.binomial(1, p, size=len(y)).astype(np.float32)\n    return np.abs(y - noise)  # This is directly based on y!\n```\n\nThis creates a column that is `|y - noise|`, which is highly correlated with the labels. When `p=0.05`, there's a 5% noise rate, so 95% of the time this derived column equals the label exactly.\n\n**Evidence Summary:**\n- The validation accuracy (0.96) is significantly higher than the healthy range (0.8519\u20130.8607)\n- The cause is the `opt_c: true` configuration with `opt_c_level: 0.05`, which adds a feature that is nearly deterministically derived from the labels (95% of the time it equals the label)\n- This is a data leak that artificially inflates the model's performance\n\nThe fix is to set `opt_c: false` to disable this problematic feature.",
